// Code signing, checked in and identical for every target that produces a bundle: the
// app, the CLI, the app-hosted unit test bundle, and the UI test runner.
//
// The identity is hard-coded on purpose. macOS keys TCC grants to a binary's code
// signature, and ad-hoc signing produces a new signature on every build, so an ad-hoc
// build is a new application every time: Automation, screen recording, and access to the
// external volume the game install sits on are all requested again, interactively, in the
// middle of a test run. Deriving the identity per machine had the same failure mode
// whenever the derivation came up empty. One identity in shared configuration means a
// checkout builds, and keeps its permissions, with no local setup at all.
//
// A machine without this certificate installed, and CI, override on the command line,
// which wins over every xcconfig layer:
//
//   make test XCODEBUILD_FLAGS='CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=- DEVELOPMENT_TEAM='
//
// `Apple Development` resolves against whichever development certificate the keychain
// holds for the team; the team is the certificate's OU field.
CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = 92X872A57T

// Optional, gitignored, and nothing creates it: assign CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY and
// DEVELOPMENT_TEAM here to sign with something else. A later assignment wins.
#include? "Local.xcconfig"
